      "summary": {
        "headline": "In fiscal year 2025, household mail use shifted across various demographic segments and overall measures.",
        "statements": [
          {
            "text": "For all households, correspondence-per-household was 98.6 pieces per household per year in FY2025, which increased by 1.4 pieces per household per year from FY2024 and decreased by 26.5 pieces per household per year from FY2020 across the FY2024 and FY2025 methodology breaks.",
            "metric": "correspondence-per-household",
            "years": [
              2020,
              2024,
              2025
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "For the $100,000-$149,999 income segment, correspondence-per-household was 97.1 pieces per household per year in FY2025, falling by 43.5 pieces per household per year from FY2020 across the FY2024 and FY2025 methodology breaks.",
            "metric": "correspondence-per-household",
            "years": [
              2020,
              2025
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "For the $150,000 and Over income segment, correspondence-per-household was 115.7 pieces per household per year in FY2025, declining by 56.2 pieces per household per year from FY2020 across the FY2024 and FY2025 methodology breaks.",
            "metric": "correspondence-per-household",
            "years": [
              2020,
              2025
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "For the 65 and Over age segment, correspondence-per-household was 130.4 pieces per household per year in FY2025, dropping by 30.9 pieces per household per year from FY2020 across the FY2024 and FY2025 methodology breaks.",
            "metric": "correspondence-per-household",
            "years": [
              2020,
              2025
            ]
          }
        ],
        "caveats": [
          "Beginning in 2010, diary data were restated because of changes in weighting methodology (Appendix A8 table notes). Values before 2010 use the earlier weighting.",
          "From the FY2024 report, results are published as pieces per household per year, and the survey contractor changed (NuStats to RCF). The FY2024 report restated 2014-2023 on its own basis, so its values for those years can differ slightly from earlier reports (e.g. bills paid by mail in 2014: 34% in FY2023, 33% in FY2024).",
          "FY2025 used a new survey design (renamed the Household Mail Survey); Q1 was still covered by the old survey. Age bands changed to Under 45 / 45-64 / 65+ (previously 18-34 / 35-54 / 55+), and 'Large MSA' was redefined in January 2025 from the top 30 metros to metros of 1 million or more."
        ]
      },
